u.s secretary of defense lloyd austin  has secured an agreement from philippine  president rodrigo duterte to restore  a key pact allowing military exercises  between the two countries to continue  american naval presence in the  indo-pacific is seen by many southeast  asian states  as a counterbalance to a rising china  beijing has claimed sovereignty over  large parts of the nearby south china  sea even  constructing artificial islands for its  navy and air force  secretary austin said his visit was  meant to underscore the importance of  the relationship with the philippines  which is the us's oldest ally in asia  joining us is journalist j.c gatinga in  manila  j.c this looks like a win for the biden  administration  is this a win for the philippines  you know melissa looking at the broader  picture of where the philippines  finds itself in in terms of its  geopolitics at the moment  it is to be considered largely also a  win for the philippines in terms  of its uh credible defense spot or its  ability to stand up against  any possible aggression from china you  see the united states is the philippines  only uh treaty military ally  and that agreement that was reaffirmed  today it's called the visiting forces  agreement  besides allowing for military exercises  between the philippines and the u.s  it actually basically allows the united  states to maintain  a considerable military presence in the  philippines that would otherwise be  prohibited under the law  now experts here in manila agree that  this visiting forces agreement  is what operationalizes it it gives  these to the alliance between the  philippines  and the us which means without it even  if there is that alliance on paper  should there be any need for the united  states to come to the philippines as aid  in a military sense  it would be very difficult to do so  given that the nearest base of the us  would probably be  japan guam or even hawaii as opposed to  having its troops and some assets  already in place in the philippines  now there's of course been a long  dispute between the philippines  and china in the south china sea and in  fact  nila took a case to the united nations  which ruled in manila's favor where do  things  uh where do things stand with those  contested waters  do we have fishermen out there do we  have military patrols out there  well in recent months the philippines  has stepped up uh  patrols in what what it calls the west  philippine sea  this is the area that the united nations  tribunal affirmed  to be the philippines as a rightful area  within the south china sea including its  200 nautical mile  exclusive economic zone and other other  territories that are disputed  by different countries in the area um  where it stands uh despite this  stepped-up presence it really was a  reaction to this increasing  number of chinese vessels that are  regularly  found present in philippine waters these  are not just  these fishing boats that the philippine  government and even experts around the  world agree to be  manned by chinese militias but also  there have been increasing reports of  chinese warships and a steady presence  of chinese coast guard vessels  in key areas of the south china sea uh  the international community and the  previous government of the philippines  right the filipino public in general  have uh  hope that this arbitral award uh that  the government would capitalize on it to  kind of push back against beijing but  the duterte administration has largely  set that aside  uh in favor of uh warmer ties with  beijing yeah i want to talk a little bit  about that  president rodrigo duterte has in the  past flattered  chinese leader xi jinping and seemed to  want to build friendlier relations with  china  where does things stand has he entirely  pivoted from that position with  the news that we're just hearing from  lloyd austin's visit  not at all melissa in fact just last  monday duterte was still added  criticizing the united states  uh his accusations basically were that  the united states have been an unfair  ally to the philippines that it has not  lived up to expectations  of what each country brings to the table  in terms of military aid of course the  philippines  is um on the weaker side of the military  spectrum  here obviously um but yeah there uh  duterte maintains a very friendly stance  against or  towards china uh he calls xi jinping his  friend in fact he wants it that he  simply loves  xi jinping and it's a consistent theme  throughout his addresses  his rhetoric whenever he's confronted  with anything relating to china he is  always  uh sounding favorable to beijing it  sounds like he's trying to  win both the united states and china and  play both sides  jc gotenga thanks so much for joining us
 

